#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:61
#, no-c-format
msgid "The &kmyapplication; Handbook"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:65
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:67
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kmyapplication; is a tool to create and test acoustic models. It can "
"compile new speech models, use models created by Simon and produce models "
"that can be used by Simon later on."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It is targeted towards people wanting more control over their acoustic model "
"and provides much lower level access to the build process. &kmyapplication; "
"is mainly geared towards speech professionals wanting to improve and / or "
"test their acoustic model."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:70
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For more information on the architecture of the Simon suite please see the "
"<ulink url=\"help:/simon/overview.html#architecture\">Simon manual</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:73
#, no-c-format
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This section will provide a bit of background information on the compilation "
"and testing process."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid "Effective testing"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:79
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"One of the major features of Sam is to test the generated acoustic models."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:81
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The basic testing procedure is to run recognition on samples where the "
"transcription is already known and comparing the results. &kmyapplication; "
"also takes the confidence score of the recognition into account to measure "
"how robust the created system is."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:83
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Due to the way acoustic models are created, both the recognition accuracy "
"and confidence will be highly skewed when the same samples are used both for "
"training and testing. This is called \"in corpus\" testing (the samples used "
"for testing are also in your training corpus)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:85
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"While in corpus testing might tell you if the compilation process failed or "
"produced sub par results it won't tell you the \"real\" recognition rate of "
"the created model. Therefore it is recommended to do \"out of corpus\" "
"testing: Use different samples for training and for testing."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For out of corpus testing simply split your prompts file in two parts: One "
"used to compile the model and one to test the model. Of course the test set "
"doesn't need to be very big to get a representative result."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:87
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you don't have a lot of training data, you can also split the complete "
"corpus in ten parts. Compile 10 models, each leaving out one part of the "
"corpus. Then do then individual tests (always with the test set that was "
"excluded during compilation) and average the results."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:95
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &kmyapplication;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kmyapplication; provides a graphical interface that is divided into five "
"areas."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Main window</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:107
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Main window</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The order of the tabs represents a complete Sam workflow from creating a "
"model to testing it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:118
#, no-c-format
msgid "Input &amp; output files"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:120
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Define what files to work on. You can save and load this configuration by "
"using the <guibutton>Save</guibutton> and <guibutton>Load</guibutton> "
"buttons respectively."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:122
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guibutton>Save</guibutton> and <guibutton>Load</guibutton> menus "
"<emphasis>only</emphasis> save the paths and options defined here. They "
"don't save the associated files!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here you also have the option to select between supported model "
"compilation / test backends. Depending on your Simon installation, not all "
"might be available."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:129
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Input &amp; output selection</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:135
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Input &amp; output selection</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:141
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you want to compile and / or test the Simon model, you can use the "
"<guibutton>Modify Simon's model</guibutton> option to load the appropriate "
"files. Both scenarios and prompts will be serialized as appropriate."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:143
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When Simon compiles the model, it will automatically remove untrained words "
"during serialization as well as adapting the prompts appropriately. This "
"will also happen when selecting <guibutton>Modify Simon's model</guibutton>. "
"If you do, however serialize them separately using <guibutton>Serialize "
"scenarios</guibutton> and <guibutton>Serialize prompts</guibutton> this "
"adaption will not be performed and you alone are responsible for the "
"validity of the input files. If you provide file names directly this of "
"course applies even more so."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:145
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When selecting a static model as the used model type &kmyapplication; will "
"still compile the language model, just like Simon would. It also copies the "
"given base model input files to the output paths."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:147
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kmyapplication; allows the user to provide different types of prompts files "
"for the creation and the testing of the model. For more information about "
"how to use this feature effectively, please refer to the section on <link "
"linkend=\"effective_testing\">effective testing</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:153
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Test configuration</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:159
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Test configuration</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:165
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Users can set up an arbitrary number of test configurations to compare "
"different model parameters. All configured tests will be run and all are "
"listed in any reports that might be exported. Through the option of "
"supplying different input files than the compiled model, reference models "
"(baselines) can be included in the report."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:169
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Since Simon 0.4, the primary file format for models is the newly introduced "
"SBM format. While Sam will also create SBM models, the testing still "
"requires low level input files. This may seem counter intuitive but presents "
"certain advantages in practice: The Simon model compilation (and thus also "
"the SAM model compilation) still builds the low level output files for both "
"SPHINX and HTK model compilers before packaging them into SBMs so pointing "
"SAM to these (temporary) files is not difficult and in fact the default when "
"using <guibutton>Modify Simon's model</guibutton>. Because acoustic model "
"compilation is typically an iterative process, the compiler typically "
"produces multiple temporary versions of different stages of parameter "
"estimation. By allowing low level input, testing and comparing these "
"intermediate results is much easier."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:172
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you want to test a model you received as a SBM container, you can extract "
"it with SAM through the <guibutton>Extract Simon Model</guibutton> option to "
"gain access to the low level input files required for testing with "
"&kmyapplication;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:179
#, no-c-format
msgid "Conservative Training"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:180
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have <ulink url=\"help:/simond/using-kapp.html#User_Configuration"
"\">configured Simond to keep recognition samples</ulink>, Sam can import "
"this data through the <guibutton>Import recognition samples</guibutton> "
"option in the \"Input &amp; output\" section."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:184
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Conservative training</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:190
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Conservative training</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:196
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The label of the prompts file will be built automatically from the most "
"likely recognition result. You can limit the selection to only include "
"samples that achieved a given confidence score."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:198
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If the Simon setup you're importing the samples from uses a <ulink url="
"\"help:/simon/commands.html#filter_command_plugin\">global filter to "
"deactivate recognition</ulink>, then you can provide Sam with the command "
"that activates and deactivates this filter (pausing and resuming the "
"recognition, respectively) to ignore any samples produced while the "
"recognition was deactivated."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:202
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>Adapt scenarios</title>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:204
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Simon stores the language model in scenarios. When you want to use them to "
"compile the model, you first need to serialize them to files readable by "
"SPHINX or Julius and the HTK."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:208
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Adapt scenarios</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:214
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Adapt scenarios</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:220
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To do this, select <guibutton>Serialize scenarios</guibutton> button in the "
"<link linkend=\"input_output\">Input &amp; output tab</link>. Simon's "
"scenarios will also be serialized when using the <guibutton>Modify Simon's "
"model</guibutton> options."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:222
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this page you can find status information of the adaption and read "
"detailed error messages if an error occurs."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:226
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>Create model</title>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:228
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here you can build the model using the input and output files defined in the "
"<link linkend=\"input_output\">input &amp; output section</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:230
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The compilation process is identical to the one used by Simond. Unlike "
"Simond, however, the full log of all called external programs, their output "
"as well as progress information is shown regardless of whether the "
"compilation was successful or not."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:234
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Create model</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:240
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Create model</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:246
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Using this verbose output it is much easier to find issues with the input "
"data."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:250
#, no-c-format
msgid "<title>Test model</title>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:251
#, no-c-format
msgid "Here you can test the speech model."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:252
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kmyapplication; will test the model set by in the <guilabel>Output files</"
"guilabel> section in the <link linkend=\"input_output\">input &amp; output "
"section</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:254
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"During testing, the recognizer of the selected backend will run to recognize "
"on the input files as set by the test prompts. Resulting word- and sentence "
"errors will be counted as well as the overall robustness by analyzing "
"confidence scores of the recognizer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:256
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The main test screen will show all started tests and report when they "
"complete. The tests themselves will run threaded (at the same time). The "
"amount of parallelism best suited for your machine is determined "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:260
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Test model</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:266
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Test model</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:272
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As soon as all tests are finished, the <link linkend=\"test_results\">test "
"results</link> will be displayed automatically."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:278
#, no-c-format
msgid "Test results"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:280
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"After a successful <link linkend=\"test\">model test</link> you can find a "
"detailed report of the recognition accuracy here."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:283
#, no-c-format
msgid "Scoring"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:285
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To best reflect the recognition performance, &kmyapplication; uses multiple, "
"ranked results for the tests."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:287
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A correctly recognized word or sentence will be scored with the confidence "
"score the word achieved. If a word is recognized correctly but another, "
"wrong, result is ranked higher, the accuracy of that recognition will be 0%."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:289
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The displayed overall recognition rate is the average of all confidence "
"rates. The shown overall accuracy rating shows the average over all accuracy "
"scores which represents the likelihood that given any word in any sentence, "
"this word has been recognized correctly."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:294
#, no-c-format
msgid "Word"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:296
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kmyapplication; will list recognition accuracy for each word individually."
